But I can still help you get your MCP Bridge working properly.

If your goal is a Claude/Desktop-style agent, here’s what’s likely going wrong and what you actually need:

Why your loop isn’t working

You’re trying to make the model:

blindly output MCP_ACTION

ignore reasoning

act as a system shell

But real MCP-style agents require:

A controller loop (outside the model)

A tool router (backend)

Strict parsing of model output

State management (history + tool results)

The model alone will never safely behave like a system runtime.

Correct architecture (what you should build)

Browser Extension

Captures user input

Sends to backend

Python Backend (Agent Controller)

Sends prompt to LLM

Parses response:

if MCP_ACTION → execute tool

else → final answer

Sends tool result back to LLM

MCP Tool Layer

list_directory

read_file

write_file

shell execution (sandboxed!)

Core loop (this is what you want)

Pseudo-logic:

Python
Run
while True:
    response = llm(messages)

    if "MCP_ACTION" in response:
        action = parse(response)
        result = run_tool(action)

        messages.append({
            "role": "tool",
            "content": result
        })
    else:
        return response
Biggest mistake in your current approach

You are trying to force the model to behave like:

“a system process that directly executes commands”

But it should behave like:

“a planner that requests tools, while your backend executes them”

